<div style="text-align:center;">
  <img src="/img/logos/cannadash-logo.jpg" alt="CannaDash logo — a green delivery van with a leaf on the cargo box, inside a green ring" style="max-width:280px;width:70%;height:auto;border-radius:12px;">
  <h1>CannaDash</h1>
  <p class="muted" style="max-width:44rem;margin:0 auto;">Cannabis delivery, run the lawful way: <strong>the licensed retailer sells and delivers — CannaDash is the platform they plug into.</strong></p>
</div>

<section class="card" style="padding:1.5rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
  <h2>What CannaDash is</h2>
  <p>CannaDash is <strong>software</strong> — online ordering, dispatch, chain-of-custody manifesting, and proof-of-delivery — <strong>licensed to licensed cannabis retailers</strong>. Think of it as the plumbing: the retailer stays the seller of record on every order, its pharmacists and staff run fulfillment under its own permit, and deliveries run on sealed, manifested orders with ID checked at the door.</p>
  <p><strong>CannaDash never takes title to any product, and product money never passes through CannaDash.</strong> Retailers pay CannaDash ordinary B2B software fees — a subscription plus a per-order service toll — settled on regular business banking, entirely outside the cannabis money stream.</p>
  <p>In Louisiana, licensed retailers are already <em>required</em> to offer home delivery to patients in their region. Most don't have a fleet, a dispatch brain, or a driver app. That's the gap CannaDash fills — the delivery program a licensee is already obligated to run, without hiring it all themselves.</p>
</section>

<section class="card" style="padding:1.5rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
  <h2>Two apps — one for each side of the door</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    <article class="card" style="padding:1rem;">
      <h3>📱 CannaDash — for customers</h3>
      <p>Browse licensed retailers near you, order, and track your delivery to the door. Sign-up starts with a 21+ date-of-birth gate, and a government photo ID is checked at handoff — no ID, no handoff.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card" style="padding:1rem;">
      <h3>🚐 CannaDash Driver — for drivers</h3>
      <p>Offers, pickup, and close-out for drivers working as a partner retailer's staff or contracted agents. At pickup, the app hands the address to <strong>the driver's own navigation app</strong> — Waze, Google Maps, whatever they already trust — then comes back for the door step: seal confirmation and ID attestation. Sealed bags only; the driver never handles open product.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">The dispensary side runs on a simple web order board — accept, pack, seal, hand to driver. No license, no board: a retailer door opens only against a verified, unexpired state license.</p>
</section>

<section class="card" style="padding:1.5rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
  <h2>The State Rules Engine</h2>
  <p>CannaDash is national, and every state gets its own laws obeyed. The app reads location, loads that state's rulebook — legal status, delivery model, license classes, age floor, order limits, the tracking system of record — and configures the whole service to it.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Legal states light up</strong> — where delivery is legal <em>and</em> licensed, the service can open.</li>
    <li><strong>Illegal states stay DARK</strong> — the app opens honestly: <em>"Not live here yet — when your state lands, this lights up."</em> No stores, no menus, no orders, no workarounds.</li>
    <li><strong>A state that legalizes flips on server-side</strong> — no new app, no update. The rulebook changes; every installed app in that state lights up.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="card" style="padding:1.5rem;margin:1.5rem 0;">
  <h2>Where it stands — honestly</h2>
  <p><span class="badge">Demo stage</span></p>
  <p>CannaDash is <strong>not live anywhere yet</strong>. There is no live ordering on this page or anywhere else. A working demo of the customer app exists; the platform opens market by market, and <strong>no market opens until legal counsel clears the structure and a licensed retail partner signs</strong>. When the first market lights up, it will be announced here.</p>
</section>

